    passageText: `G protein–coupled receptors (GPCRs) constitute the largest family of membrane receptors in mammals. Upon ligand binding, GPCRs activate heterotrimeric G proteins (composed of Gα, Gβ, and Gγ subunits) by facilitating exchange of GDP for GTP on the Gα subunit. The activated Gα·GTP dissociates from Gβγ and modulates downstream effectors. Gαs stimulates adenylyl cyclase, increasing intracellular cAMP; Gαi inhibits adenylyl cyclase; Gαq activates phospholipase C (PLC), which generates inositol-1,4,5-trisphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG).

Elevated cAMP activates protein kinase A (PKA). In its inactive state, PKA is a holoenzyme consisting of two regulatory (R) subunits and two catalytic (C) subunits. Binding of four cAMP molecules (two per R subunit) causes dissociation of the R₂C₂ complex, releasing two active catalytic subunits. Free C subunits phosphorylate serine/threonine residues on target proteins, including CREB (cAMP response element-binding protein) in the nucleus, glycogen phosphorylase kinase, and phospholamban (in cardiac muscle).

Signal termination occurs through multiple mechanisms: phosphodiesterases hydrolyze cAMP to 5′-AMP; protein phosphatases dephosphorylate PKA substrates; and the intrinsic GTPase activity of Gα hydrolyzes GTP to GDP, re-associating Gα with Gβγ and returning the heterotrimeric G protein to its inactive state.`,
