    title: 'Protein Folding, Chaperones, and Proteasomal Degradation',
    passageText: `Proteins must fold into precise three-dimensional conformations to perform their biological functions. Folding is thermodynamically driven by burial of hydrophobic residues away from water (hydrophobic collapse) and formation of intramolecular hydrogen bonds, van der Waals interactions, and (where present) disulfide bonds. The native conformation represents the lowest free energy state accessible under physiological conditions, not necessarily the global energy minimum — local minima ("kinetic traps") can cause misfolding.

Molecular chaperones assist folding by preventing premature aggregation. Heat shock proteins (Hsp70 and Hsp90) recognize exposed hydrophobic patches on unfolded or partially folded proteins and bind them in an ATP-dependent manner. Hsp70 holds substrate proteins while preventing aggregation; transfer to the barrel-shaped GroEL/GroES complex (Hsp60 family) provides an isolated chamber for folding. Chaperone expression is upregulated by the heat shock response (transcription factor HSF1) during cellular stress (heat, oxidative stress, misfolded protein accumulation).

Misfolded proteins that cannot be rescued by chaperones are targeted for proteasomal degradation. Ubiquitin-activating enzyme (E1) activates ubiquitin, transfers it to ubiquitin-conjugating enzyme (E2), which works with ubiquitin ligase (E3) to attach polyubiquitin chains to lysine residues of the target protein. The 26S proteasome (comprising a 20S proteolytic core and 19S regulatory caps) recognizes the polyubiquitin tag, unfolds the protein, translocates it into the 20S core, and degrades it to small peptides. These peptides can be presented on MHC-I molecules for immune surveillance.`,
